      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\), we first identify the standard form of the hyperbola equation.\n\nThe standard form of a hyperbola that opens left and right is:\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\nBy comparing this with the given equation \\(\\frac{x^2}{99} - \\frac{y^2}{36} = 1\\), we identify:\n\\[\na^2 = 99 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b^2 = 36\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the vertices of a hyperbola is given by \\(2a\\). Therefore, to find \\(2a\\), we take the square root of \\(a^2\\):\n\\[\na^2 = 99\n\\]\nSince \\(a^2 = 99\\), we have:\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{99}\n\\]\n\nNow, the distance between the vertices is:\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\sqrt{99}\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(\\sqrt{99}\\) in a simplified form, we look for the prime factorization:\n\\[\n99 = 3^2 \\times 11\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{99} = \\sqrt{3^2 \\times 11} = 3 \\sqrt{11}\n\\]\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3 \\sqrt{11} = 6 \\sqrt{11}\n\\]\n\nSo, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{11}}\n\\]",
